{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nAs behooves any intelligent schoolboy, Kevin Sun is studying psycowlogy, cowculus, and cryptcowgraphy at the Bovinia State University (BGU) under Farmer Ivan. During his Mathematics of Olympiads (MoO) class, Kevin was confronted with a weird functional equation and needs your help. For two fixed integers k and p, where p is an odd prime number, the functional equation states that  for some function . (This equation should hold for any integer x in the range 0 to p\u2009-\u20091, inclusive.)It turns out that f can actually be many different functions. Instead of finding a solution, Kevin wants you to count the number of distinct functions f that satisfy this equation. Since the answer may be very large, you should print your result modulo 109\u2009+\u20097.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 2 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n[p, k] = [int(x) for x in raw_input().split()]\nord_k = 1\nwhile pow(k, ord_k, p) != 1:\n    ord_k += 1\ncomps = (p - 1) / ord_k\nmod = 1000000007\nif k == 1:\n    print pow(p, p, mod)\nelse:\n    print pow(p, comps, mod)\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\ndef solve():\n    p, k = map(int, raw_input().split())\n    mod = 1000000007\n    \n    # Handle special case when k == 0\n    if k == 0:\n        # Equation: f(0) = f(0) + f(0) + ... (but actually k=0 means f(x) = f(0) for all x)\n        # Actually for k=0: f(x) = f(0) for all x, so we have p choices for f(0), and all other values are determined\n        # But wait, let's reconsider the problem.\n        # The functional equation is f(x) = f(k*x mod p) for all x\n        # When k=0: f(x) = f(0) for all x != 0, and f(0) = f(0) (always true)\n        # So we can choose f(0) freely (p choices), and f(1)=f(2)=...=f(p-1)=f(0)\n        # So total p functions\n        print p % mod\n        return\n    \n    # When k=1: f(x) = f(x) always true, so any function works: p^p possibilities\n    if k == 1:\n        print pow(p, p, mod)\n        return\n    \n    # For k != 0,1: we need to find the multiplicative order of k modulo p\n    # The functional equation is f(x) = f(k*x mod p)\n    # For x=0: f(0) = f(0) always true\n    # For x != 0: we're working in the multiplicative group modulo p\n    \n    # Find the order of k in the multiplicative group Z_p*\n    ord_k = 1\n    current = k % p\n    while current != 1:\n        ord_k += 1\n        current = (current * k) % p\n        if ord_k > p:  # Safety check\n            break\n    \n    # The multiplicative group has p-1 elements\n    # The orbits under multiplication by k have size ord_k\n    # So number of orbits in Z_p* is (p-1) / ord_k\n    # Plus the orbit {0} which is fixed\n    # Total number of equivalence classes = 1 + (p-1)/ord_k\n    \n    # Each equivalence class can be assigned any value independently\n    # So total number of functions = p^(number of equivalence classes)\n    \n    num_classes = 1 + (p - 1) // ord_k\n    print pow(p, num_classes, mod)\n\nsolve()\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.95\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-3.576278118089249e-07, -4.279521817807108e-05, -2.7418097943154862e-06, -5.960462772236497e-07, -0.22199390828609467, -0.1183418408036232, -3.075552376685664e-05, -3.576272320060525e-06, -0.14644202589988708, -0.0003073934931308031, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -3.933898824470816e-06, -0.20188689231872559, -5.960462772236497e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -5.578839045483619e-05, -8.344646857949556e-07, -8.344646857949556e-07, -1.0132738680113107e-05, -3.731181277544238e-05, -0.5231287479400635, -0.19779346883296967, -5.960462772236497e-07, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, -0.020546572282910347, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0, -7.152555099310121e-07, -4.291525328881107e-06, -0.000710592488758266, -0.012891406193375587, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.28115084767341614, -0.5888264775276184, -0.07422368973493576, -0.07891497761011124, -0.07872415333986282, -0.17974753677845, -0.09292294830083847, -8.344646857949556e-07, -0.0028048718813806772, -0.023186277598142624, -4.887569048150908e-06, -0.0031784274615347385, -2.50339189733495e-06, -0.04299934208393097, 0.0, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -5.960462772236497e-07, -1.5497195136049413e-06, -6.198863957251888e-06, -0.49484312534332275, -0.35736408829689026, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.04301783815026283, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.0007360848248936236, -1.966933996300213e-05, -0.020141245797276497, -0.008766734972596169, -5.960462772236497e-07, -0.01637226901948452, -0.2545854449272156, -0.4379754364490509, -0.31372326612472534, -4.6491513785440475e-06, -3.2066785934148356e-05, -0.2545091211795807, -2.9802276912960224e-06, -0.29107898473739624, -0.7208981513977051, -0.105165496468544, -0.965234100818634, -0.001384015311487019, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -0.016770802438259125, -0.23889382183551788, -0.14275766909122467, -0.0012940136948600411, -1.966933996300213e-05, -0.000321336614433676, -2.9444261599564925e-05, 0.0, -0.0016563518438488245, -0.0009266611887142062, -0.0002162222663173452, 